F={
"sumer":"Barley was the staple: baked as flat bread and, above all, brewed into thick beer drunk through straws, with temple workers paid daily rations of bread and beer. Dates, onions, garlic, lentils, chickpeas, fish from canals and marshes, and mutton for the wealthy filled out the diet; the Yale culinary tablets (~1750 BCE, Babylonian) preserve the oldest written recipes, mostly stews.",
"akkadian-empire":"Like Sumer, Akkad lived on irrigated barley and emmer, turned into bread and beer, with dates, sesame oil, onions and fish. State workshops issued standard rations, and sheep and goats supplied wool, milk and meat for feasts.",
"babylonia":"Babylonians ate barley bread, beer, dates, lentils, onions, leeks and garlic, cooked with sesame oil; the Yale tablets record some 25 stews of lamb, pigeon and vegetables seasoned with mint and coriander. Fish and dates were cheap staples, and the Code of Hammurabi even regulated beer sellers.",
"assyria":"Assyrians farmed barley and wheat in rain-watered northern Mesopotamia and kept sheep and cattle. Ashurnasirpal II's banquet stele boasts of feeding 69,574 guests with oxen, sheep, deer, fowl, fish, bread, beer and 10,000 skins of wine.",
"ancient-egypt":"Bread and beer made from emmer and barley were so basic that wages were paid in them; onions, garlic, lentils, chickpeas, lettuce and Nile fish were everyday foods. The rich ate beef, roast fowl, figs, pomegranates, honey cakes and wine; tomb paintings show bakeries and breweries in detail.",
"hittite-empire":"Hittites ate barley and emmer bread in dozens of named shapes, porridge, lentils, figs, grapes, honey and cheese. Beer and wine were offered to gods, and royal storage pits at Hattusa could hold grain for thousands of people for a year.",
"phoenicia":"Phoenicians ate barley and wheat bread, olives and olive oil, fish, figs and dates, and traded wine across the Mediterranean in amphorae. Their colonists spread vine and olive cultivation to North Africa and Spain.",
"ancient-israel-and-judah":"The Seven Species of the Bible — wheat, barley, grapes, figs, pomegranates, olives and dates (honey) — were the basis of the diet. Most people ate bread, lentil stews, cheese and yoghurt from goats and sheep; meat was for festivals and sacrifices, and dietary laws (kashrut) defined permitted animals.",
"kingdom-of-kush":"Kushites grew sorghum, millet and barley along the Nile and kept large herds of cattle, which were symbols of wealth. They also ate fish, dates and dom palm fruit and drank sorghum beer.",
"carthage":"Carthaginians farmed wheat, barley, olives, grapes and pomegranates (the 'Punic apple') on rich estates, guided by Mago's farming manual. They ate puls-like porridge, fish and garum, and Romans copied their recipe for placenta cake.",
"achaemenid-persian-empire":"Persians ate wheat bread, rice in some regions, mutton, fruit such as pomegranates, apricots and pistachios, and drank wine. Greek writers marvelled at the royal table, which fed up to 15,000 people a day, and at desserts served throughout the meal.",
"aksum":"Teff, the tiny grain used for injera flatbread, and barley were staples, with cattle, sheep and honey. Aksum also traded for spices and wine with India and Rome.",
"ghana-empire":"People grew millet, sorghum and rice in the Sahel and herded cattle, sheep and goats. Salt, traded from Saharan mines, was so valuable it was exchanged weight for weight with gold in some accounts.",
"mali-empire":"Mali's farmers grew millet, sorghum and African rice along the Niger, and used shea butter as cooking oil. Ibn Battuta (1352) described millet porridge, fish and baobab sauce.",
"songhai-empire":"Songhai farmers grew rice, millet and sorghum in the Niger bend floodplain; Sorko fishermen supplied fish. Cattle-herding Fulani supplied milk and butter.",
"great-zimbabwe":"Cattle were the main wealth and source of meat and milk, with sorghum, finger millet and beans grown on hillsides.",
"swahili-city-states":"Swahili towns ate rice, millet, coconut, bananas, fish and spices traded across the Indian Ocean.",
"kingdom-of-benin":"Yams were the central crop, eaten with palm-oil soups, plantains and bushmeat; kola nuts were shared in hospitality.",
"ottoman-empire":"Ottoman palace kitchens at Topkapı fed up to 10,000 people a day; dishes included pilaf, kebabs, börek, dolma, yoghurt and baklava. Coffee houses spread from Istanbul in the 16th century.",
"safavid-iran":"Rice dishes (polow and chelow) with saffron, herbs and fruit, kebabs, flatbreads and sherbets; tea and coffee spread later.",
"indus-valley-civilisation":"Indus farmers grew wheat, barley, millets, peas, lentils, sesame and cotton, and ate dates and mangoes; residues in pots show stews of meat and dairy. Cattle, buffalo, goat and fish were common, and grinding stones, ovens and large granaries suggest organised food storage.",
"vedic-india":"The Rigvedic diet was based on barley (yava), milk, curd, ghee and honey, with meat at sacrifices; later rice (vrihi) grew in the Ganga valley. Soma, a ritual drink, was praised in hymns.",
"magadha":"Magadha's wealth rested on wet rice cultivation in the Ganga plain, along with pulses, sugarcane and fish.",
"maurya-empire":"People ate rice, wheat, barley, pulses, sugarcane, dairy and fruit; Megasthenes noted rice eaten from golden bowls. The Arthashastra set rules on storehouses, fair prices and famine relief.",
"satavahana-dynasty":"Rice in coastal areas, millets in the Deccan, pulses and dairy; pepper and textiles were exported to Rome.",
"sangam-era-tamilakam":"Tamils ate rice, millet, fish, meat and fruit; Sangam poems describe five landscapes each with its own food. Black pepper was exported to Rome.",
"kushan-empire":"Wheat, barley, grapes, melons and wine in Bactria; rice and dairy in India.",
"gupta-empire":"Rice, wheat, pulses, milk products, sugar (crystallised sugar was an Indian invention) and spices; Faxian noted that many people avoided meat, onions and alcohol.",
"pallava-dynasty":"Rice, millets, coconuts, pepper and fish.",
"chalukya-dynasty":"Millets (jowar, ragi), rice and pulses.",
"rashtrakuta-dynasty":"Jowar and other millets, rice, pulses and dairy.",
"chola-empire":"Rice from the Kaveri delta, coconut, tamarind, pepper and fish; temple kitchens served thousands.",
"pala-empire":"Rice, fish, pulses and sweets made from milk.",
"delhi-sultanate":"Wheat bread, rice, meat dishes such as samosa (sanbusa) and pilaf, and sweets; Persian cuisine mixed with Indian.",
"vijayanagara-empire":"Rice, ragi, pulses and spices; Domingo Paes described markets full of food.",
"ahom-kingdom":"Rice, fish, bamboo shoots and rice beer.",
"mughal-empire":"Mughal kitchens blended Persian, Central Asian and Indian cooking: biryani, pulao, kebabs, kormas, naan and kulfi. Babur missed Kabul's melons; ordinary people ate khichdi, roti and dal.",
"maratha-confederacy":"Bhakri of jowar or bajra, dal, onion and chillies — a portable diet for fast cavalry.",
"sikh-empire":"Wheat roti, dairy, and the langar community kitchen that fed everyone equally.",
"kingdom-of-mysore":"Ragi balls (ragi mudde), rice and sambar.",
"east-india-company-rule":"Indians ate traditional grains and pulses, but Company revenue demands contributed to famines such as the Bengal famine of 1770, which killed about a third of the province's people.",
"british-raj":"Most Indians ate rice or wheat, pulses and vegetables; famines in 1876–78, 1896–1902 and 1943 killed tens of millions while grain was exported.",
"xia-and-shang-china":"Millet in the north and rice in the south, plus pigs, dogs and fermented alcoholic drinks made in bronze vessels.",
"zhou-dynasty":"Millet, wheat, soybeans, vegetables and meat for nobles; elaborate ritual banquets were described in the Book of Rites.",
"qin-dynasty":"Millet and wheat, with state granaries feeding armies.",
"han-dynasty":"Millet, wheat (as noodles), rice, soybeans and tofu (traditionally Han), plus pork and tea.",
"tang-dynasty":"Rice, wheat cakes, mutton and tea, which became a national drink; foreign foods such as grapes and sesame cakes were popular in Chang'an.",
"song-dynasty":"Champa rice, tea, restaurants and teahouses in cities; the first known restaurant menus.",
"yuan-dynasty":"Mongol mutton, dairy and soups combined with Chinese rice dishes.",
"ming-dynasty":"Rice and new American crops: maize, sweet potatoes and chillies.",
"qing-dynasty":"Rice, maize and the Manchu–Han imperial feast.",
"goguryeo-baekje-and-silla":"Rice, barley and fermented vegetables.",
"joseon-korea":"Rice, kimchi (chilli was added in the 18th century) and many banchan side dishes.",
"heian-japan":"Rice, fish and vegetables; Buddhism discouraged meat.",
"edo-japan-tokugawa":"Rice, miso, tofu; sushi and tempura stalls in Edo.",
"funan":"Rice from the Mekong delta and fish.",
"srivijaya":"Rice, sago, fish and spices.",
"khmer-empire":"Rice, fish (prahok paste) and fruit.",
"majapahit":"Rice, spices and coconut.",
"polynesian-voyagers":"Voyagers carried 'canoe plants' — taro, breadfruit, yam, banana, sugarcane, coconut and later sweet potato — plus pigs, dogs and chickens. Fish and shellfish provided protein.",
"minoan-civilisation":"Olives, wine, honey, barley, legumes and fish; storage jars (pithoi) filled palace magazines.",
"mycenaean-greece":"Barley, wheat, olive oil, figs and wine; Linear B tablets record rations.",
"classical-greece":"Bread or barley porridge, olives, cheese, figs and wine mixed with water; meat came mainly from sacrifices. Spartans ate a notorious black broth.",
"macedonian-empire":"Greek foods spread across the east; Alexander's troops encountered sugarcane ('honey without bees') in India.",
"roman-republic":"Puls (wheat porridge), bread, olives, cheese and vegetables; garum fish sauce.",
"roman-empire":"Bread was so central that the state distributed free grain (the annona) to about 200,000 Roman citizens; Egyptian grain fleets fed the capital. Wealthy Romans dined reclining on dormice, oysters and peacock, recorded in Apicius' cookbook.",
"byzantine-empire":"Bread, fish, olive oil, cheese, wine and fasting foods for Orthodox fasts.",
"celts":"Wheat, barley, pork, beer and mead.",
"vikings":"Porridge, bread, dried fish, meat and dairy; ale and mead.",
"holy-roman-empire":"Bread, beer, cabbage and pork.",
"kievan-rus":"Rye bread, kasha and kvass.",
"spanish-empire":"The Columbian Exchange brought maize, potatoes, tomatoes and chocolate to Europe and wheat and cattle to the Americas.",
"portuguese-empire":"Spice trade; chillies brought from America to India.",
"british-empire":"Tea, sugar and spices were traded; slave plantations produced sugar.",
"russian-empire":"Rye bread, cabbage soup (shchi) and kasha.",
"soviet-union":"Bread, potatoes and cabbage; famines in 1932–33 killed millions.",
"norte-chico-caral":"Anchovies and sardines from the coast traded for squash, beans and cotton.",
"olmec":"Maize processed with lime (nixtamalisation), beans and squash.",
"maya-civilisation":"Maize was sacred; people ate tortillas, tamales and atole, with beans, squash, chilli and turkey. Elites drank frothy cacao.",
"teotihuacan":"Maize, beans, squash, amaranth and pulque.",
"aztec-empire":"Maize tortillas, beans, squash, chilli, amaranth and chinampa vegetables; nobles drank chocolate.",
"inca-empire":"Potatoes (freeze-dried as chuño), maize, quinoa and llama meat; storehouses (qollqa) held food for years.",
"ancestral-puebloans":"Maize, beans and squash, plus wild game.",
"mississippian-culture":"Maize, beans and squash.",
"haudenosaunee-confederacy":"The Three Sisters: maize, beans and squash planted together.",
"united-states":"Corn, wheat and beef; fast food spread worldwide in the 20th century.",
}
